### What are Java Records?

Records replace POJO boilerplate with a single line.

### What does a record generate?

```java
// One line:
public record AccountSummary(String accountId, java.math.BigDecimal balance, String status) {}

// Compiler generates (equivalent to ~50 lines of POJO):
// - private final fields: accountId, balance, status
// - Canonical constructor
// - Accessors: accountId(), balance(), status() (not getAccountId)
// - equals() based on all components
// - hashCode() based on all components
// - toString(): AccountSummary[accountId=SAV2001, balance=125000, status=ACTIVE]
```

### How do you add validation?

```java
public record AccountSummary(String accountId, java.math.BigDecimal balance, String status) {
    // Compact constructor: validation without repeating assignments
    public AccountSummary {
        if (accountId == null || accountId.isBlank()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("accountId required");
        if (balance.signum() < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("balance cannot be negative");
        // No this.accountId = accountId needed: compiler adds it.
    }

    // Custom methods
    public boolean isHighValue() {
        return balance.compareTo(new java.math.BigDecimal("100000")) > 0;
    }

    // Static factory
    public static AccountSummary of(String id, double amount) {
        return new AccountSummary(id, java.math.BigDecimal.valueOf(amount), "ACTIVE");
    }
}
```

### How do records enforce OOP principles?

```java
// Encapsulation: fields are private final, no setters
AccountSummary summary = new AccountSummary("SAV2001", new java.math.BigDecimal("125000"), "ACTIVE");
// summary.accountId = "HACKED"; // compile error: private final
// No setAccountId() exists.

// Value semantics: same data = equal
AccountSummary s1 = new AccountSummary("SAV2001", new java.math.BigDecimal("125000"), "ACTIVE");
AccountSummary s2 = new AccountSummary("SAV2001", new java.math.BigDecimal("125000"), "ACTIVE");
s1.equals(s2); // true: same component values
s1.hashCode() == s2.hashCode(); // true: works in HashSet/HashMap

// Immutability: safe across threads, no synchronization needed
```

### When are records NOT suitable?

```java
// Not for JPA entities:
// @Entity public record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}
// 1. JPA requires no-arg constructor: records do not have one.
// 2. JPA needs setters for dirty checking: records are immutable.
// 3. JPA proxies need non-final class: records are final.

// Not for mutable domain objects:
// You need account.setBalance(newBalance): records do not support this.

// Not for inheritance hierarchies:
// Records are final. class SpecialTrade extends Trade { } // compile error.

// Perfect for:
// DTOs: record TradeResponse(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount, String status) {}
// Value objects: record Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {}
// Map keys: record CompositeKey(String accountId, java.time.LocalDate date) {}
// Event payloads: record TradeExecutedEvent(String tradeId, BigDecimal amount) {}
```

### How do records compare to regular classes?

| Aspect | Record | Regular Class |
|--------|--------|---------------|
| Mutability | Immutable (private final) | Configurable |
| Boilerplate | One line | 50+ lines |
| Inheritance | Cannot extend (final) | Can extend |
| Interfaces | Can implement | Can implement |
| equals/hashCode | Auto (all components) | Manual |
| JPA compatible | No | Yes |
| Use case | DTOs, value objects | Entities, services |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Spring Boot, records work for @ConfigurationProperties and request/response DTOs.
- Records with sealed interfaces create algebraic data types: sealed interface TransactionResult permits Success, Failure { }.
- The compact constructor eliminates a common bug: forgetting to include a field in equals() or hashCode().
